# For a plain Windows machine running BOTH the backend and the Expo dev server (no WSL2 involved).
# Copy this file to .env.local and fill in your own PC's LAN IP:
#
#   copy .env.windows.example .env.local
#
# Steps:
#   1. Find your PC's LAN IP: open Command Prompt / PowerShell and run
#        ipconfig
#      Look for "IPv4 Address" under your active Wi-Fi or Ethernet adapter, e.g. 192.168.1.42.
#      (Not 127.0.0.1 / localhost - Expo Go on your phone cannot reach your PC that way.)
#
#   2. Allow the backend's port through Windows Defender Firewall the first time you run it - either
#      click "Allow access" on the firewall popup, or add a rule manually:
#        Windows Defender Firewall -> Advanced settings -> Inbound Rules -> New Rule -> Port -> TCP 5000 -> Allow.
#
#   3. Put that IP below, then restart both `npm run dev` (backend) and `npx expo start` (this app) -
#      EXPO_PUBLIC_* variables are inlined at start time, not read live.
#
#   4. Your phone and this PC must be on the SAME Wi-Fi network. If it still can't connect, some
#      networks isolate devices from each other (AP/client isolation) - try a different network, your
#      phone's own mobile hotspot, or `npx expo start --tunnel`.
EXPO_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://192.168.1.42:5000
